# 2. Huayan and Madhyamaka

Huayan accepts the basic Madhyamaka insight:

> **Whatever arises dependently is empty of intrinsic nature.**

Madhyamaka applies this without exception. Bodies, objects, selves, consciousness, causation, emptiness and even Buddhist doctrines lack independent *svabhāva*. Candrakīrti’s criticism of Yogācāra is precisely that showing the objects of consciousness to be conditioned is insufficient if consciousness itself is then retained as a metaphysical foundation. ([Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy][2])

Madhyamaka is therefore usually understood as **anti-foundational**:

* there is no final substance beneath appearances;
* no independently real consciousness supports the world;
* emptiness is not a hidden Absolute;
* ultimate truth cannot be captured as an entity or theory.

Its two truths distinguish ordinary, conventionally functioning reality from the insight that phenomena do not arise through intrinsic natures. Even this ultimate insight is not an independently existing object. ([Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy][2])

## Huayan’s difference

Huayan asks:

> Once everything has been emptied of independent nature, what does the world look like positively?

Its answer is not merely absence. Emptiness means **unlimited relational openness**.

A thing without a sealed intrinsic essence can:

* receive determination from everything else;
* contribute to the determination of everything else;
* contain the whole relational order from its own perspective;
* manifest the same emptiness/suchness as every other thing.

So Madhyamaka tends to use emptiness primarily to **deconstruct reification**, while Huayan uses emptiness to construct a vision of **total interdependence and mutual inclusion**.

This does not mean Nāgārjuna denied interdependence. Rather, Huayan develops its ontological and phenomenological implications much more positively and elaborately.

A useful progression is:

> **Madhyamaka:** no thing exists from itself.
> **Huayan:** therefore every thing exists through all things.

The famous Mahāyāna formula is:

> *rūpaṃ śūnyatā śūnyataiva rūpam*
> “Form is emptiness; emptiness itself is form.”

Huayan radicalizes it:

> **Every form is emptiness; therefore every form is open to and present within every other form.**

