# 3. Huayan and Yogācāra

The Reddit post oversimplifies Yogācāra when it says:

> “Everything stems from the ālayavijñāna.”

That is one possible schematic summary, but it can make the ālayavijñāna sound like Brahman or a cosmic creator-consciousness. Classical Yogācāra usually presents it more carefully.

## What ālayavijñāna does

The store or substratum consciousness explains:

* continuity without an eternal self;
* the preservation of karmic tendencies;
* how earlier actions condition later experiences;
* how a structured world appears through the maturation of karmic “seeds.”

It was developed partly to resolve problems created by combining no-self with the momentariness of conditioned events. ([Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy][3])

It is generally **conditioned, karmically structured and capable of transformation**. It is not straightforwardly an eternal pure consciousness.

## The three natures

Yogācāra analyses experience through three natures:

### Imagined nature — *parikalpita*

The falsely projected division into inherently existing subjects and objects:

> “Here is a self-existing observer; over there is a self-existing world.”

### Dependent nature — *paratantra*

The actual causal flow of experience arising from conditions, habits, karmic seeds and cognition.

### Perfected nature — *pariniṣpanna*

The dependent flow as realized to be empty of the imagined subject-object duality.

The perfected nature is not necessarily a separate substance. It is commonly defined as **the absence of the imagined nature within the dependent nature**. Nevertheless, some Yogācāra interpretations give the nondual dependent flow a stronger foundational status than Madhyamaka permits. ([Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy][3])

## Is Yogācāra idealism?

That remains debated.

Some readings say Yogācāra teaches ontological idealism: only consciousness ultimately exists. Others read it as phenomenology or epistemic discipline:

> Do not make claims about a mind-independent world because everything you know is presented within cognition.

Even Vasubandhu’s arguments against external objects can be read either as denying them outright or as showing that their existence cannot be established through perception. ([Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy][3])

So one should avoid saying that **all Yogācāra simply teaches that a cosmic ālaya produces everything**.

