# 6. Is One Mind basically Brahman?

This is the hardest question.

There is an undeniable resemblance:

* one all-pervading reality;
* phenomena inseparable from that reality;
* positive language such as purity, eternity and bliss;
* liberation through recognizing one’s true nature.

That is why phrases such as “transpersonal self” appear in some modern interpretations of Huayan. ([Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy][1])

But Huayan tries to block a Brahman-like substantialist interpretation in several ways.

### One Mind is not an individual subject

It is not a cosmic person thinking the universe into existence.

### It is not separate from phenomena

There is no pure substance behind the world that could remain what it is without its manifestations.

### Its “oneness” is nondual, not numerical

“One Mind” does not mean one enormous object instead of many small objects. Numerical oneness already contrasts one with many. Huayan instead claims that one and many are mutually constitutive.

### It is empty

Even Buddha-nature cannot possess an isolated self-nature. Its universality consists precisely in not being bounded, private or self-subsistent.

So the safest conclusion is:

> **Huayan comes unusually close to an Absolute-style metaphysics, but its Absolute is supposed to be empty, relational and non-separate from the conditioned world—not an independently existing substrate.**

Whether Huayan completely avoids reifying One Mind remains a real philosophical dispute. A strict Madhyamaka thinker may say Huayan has smuggled a foundation back into emptiness. A Huayan thinker replies that One Mind is not an entity beneath relations; it is the entire empty-relational reality viewed without fragmentation.

